==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|theosophie||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{m|la|theosophia}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|θεοσοφῐ́ᾱ||knowledge of things divine}}, from {{compound|grc|θεός|σοφῐ́ᾱ|t1=god|t2=wisdom|nocat=1}}; analogous to {{confix|en|theo|sophy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|[θɪˈɒsəfɪ]}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|[θi.ˈɒs.ə.fi]}}
* {{hyphenation|en|the|os|o|phy}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|philosophy|religion}} any doctrine of religious philosophy and mysticism claiming that knowledge of [[God]] can be attained through [[mystical]] [[insight]] and spiritual ecstasy, and that direct communication with the [[transcendent]] world is possible.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1984|last=Lee|first=Tanith|authorlink=Tanith Lee|title=Bite-Me-Not, or Fleur de Feu|pages=|magazine={{w|Isaac Asimov's Science Fiction Magazine}}|editor=|month=Oct|volume=8|location=|publisher=Davis Publications|issn=0162-2188|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=sNhaAAAAYAAJ|passage=They have their own traditions of art and science. They do not {{...}} discuss God or metaphysics or men. {{...}} All their wisdom and '''theosophy''', and all their grasp of beauty, truth or love, is in the singing. {{...}} They look unloving enough, and so they are. Pitiless fallen angels.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|religion}} Any system which claims to attain communication with God and superior spirits by physical processes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|last=Szulakowska|first=Urszula|authorlink=|title=The alchemy of light : geometry and optics in late Renaissance alchemical illustration|location=Leiden [u.a.]|publisher=Brill|isbn=9789004116900|page=11|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZJox8Eh-gs8C&pg=PA11|passage=The religious ideas of late Renaissance [[alchemist]]s were based on the Paracelsian theosophical system, which was a half-[[pagan]], half-[[Christian]] belief system, both a mystical path and a [[theurgic]] practice.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# The system of beliefs and doctrines of the {{w|Theosophical Society}}.

